.TH BMATRIX 1 "September 2026" "bmatrix 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
bmatrix \- animate concealed images through a Matrix-style ASCII field
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bmatrix
.RI [ options ] " image.png"
.br
.B bmatrix --demo
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Displays PNG or binary PPM (P6) images through a continuously falling field of
printable ASCII glyphs. Classic Matrix green is the default. Image aspect ratio
is preserved, but its detail is intentionally carried indirectly by the density
and brightness of the field.
The default field uses CMatrix's normal printable character range and every-second
column spacing; a dim image layer remains beneath the moving streams.
No terminal graphics protocol or special font is required.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --demo
Use a generated demonstration image.
.TP
.BI --mode " green|mono|full"
Select the artistic palette, independently of terminal color capability.
.TP
.BI --color " auto|none|16|256|truecolor"
Override terminal color capability detection.
.TP
.BI --size " COLSxROWS"
Set the output dimensions.
.TP
.BI --frames " N"
Exit after a bounded number of frames.
.TP
.BI --fps " N"
Set the animation frame rate.
.TP
.BI --duration " SECONDS"
Set the legacy downward reveal duration.
.TP
.BI --seed " N"
Set the deterministic effect seed.
.TP
.BI --effect " matrix|flow|none"
Select a perpetual Matrix field, the legacy downward reveal, or a static image.
Matrix is the default.
.TP
.BI --image-strength " N"
Set the concealed image strength from 0 to 1. The default is 0.7.
.TP
.BI --progress " FRACTION"
Render a deterministic legacy reveal position between 0 and 1.
.TP
.BI --time " SECONDS"
Render a deterministic Matrix-field snapshot at the given time.
.TP
.BI --output " plain|json"
Write a static frame for inspection or testing without terminal control codes.
.TP
.B --help
Show all supported options.
.TP
.B --version
Print the program version.
.SH CONTROLS
Press q to quit. Space pauses or resumes animation. Press r to replay.
.SH NOTES
Terminal dimensions, decoded images, and memory use are bounded.
Capability overrides are intended for terminals whose environment does not
accurately describe color support. Some terminals can only display a reduced palette.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero indicates success. A nonzero status indicates invalid arguments or an error.
